einforce a Likely Democratic rating, though North Carolina's narrow partisan lean keeps some uncertainty.", "watch": "A late tightening, renewed Republican outside spending, or a shift in the national environment could bring this back toward a single-digit tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's open Senate seat has become Democrats' top pickup opportunity of the cycle. Former Governor Roy Cooper leads Republican Michael Whatley consistently, with recent polls from both independent and Republican-leaning firms showing margins from five to thirteen points. Polling averages sit around eight to ten points in Cooper's favor.\n\nCooper has also built a large financial advantage, raising roughly three times Whatley's total, while Republican super PACs have reportedly cut millions in planned advertising. National raters uniformly rate the race as a Democratic lean or better, and prediction markets price Cooper's chances above ninety percent. Whatley retains backing from Trump and Vance, but the evidence points to a clear, though not insurmountable, Cooper edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9425, "margin": 7.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.93, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9375, "margin": 8.2}]}